WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.366 --> 00:00:11.580
Captain Jeff RANDALL: Imagine a place that is&nbsp;
very white. And all you see around you is ice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:21.960
All you see is vastness. All you see is&nbsp;
great expanses of nothing. The ice never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:32.100
seems to end. It goes on forever. It's an&nbsp;
area of immense beauty or immense loneliness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:42.240
That is the region that is the Arctic.
Gabrielle SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:52.560
The Arctic. It sits at the top of the globe, is&nbsp;
covered in snow and sea ice and has days without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:01:00.540
sunlight. We think of it in terms of extremes&nbsp;
- harsh, cold, barren. But the Arctic is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:06.900
home to 4 million people, endangered species and&nbsp;
a wealth of minerals and oil buried in the earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:13.800
For centuries it was inaccessible to anyone but&nbsp;
the most daring explorers and scientists. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:19.860
as climate change takes its toll, the ice&nbsp;
is melting fast, new routes are opening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.860
and the world could be facing new competition&nbsp;
over Arctic riches. To complicate matters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:32.760
no single country actually owns the place. The&nbsp;
U.S. has a portion through Alaska. Russia has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:38.580
large sector of Arctic territory in the far North,&nbsp;
and six other countries have territories of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:44.940
own. And in the middle is a vast section that&nbsp;
belongs to everyone, and no one. I’m Gabrielle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:51.900
Sierra and this is Why It Matters. Today, the&nbsp;
Arctic, where diplomatic relations are teetering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:59.700
on thin ice.
Esther BRIMMER:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:06.960
The Arctic, you should think of as a&nbsp;
frozen ocean surrounded by land. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:12.840
they're basically two big land masses. 
SIERRA: This is Esther Brimmer, Senior Fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:18.060
in Global Governance here at the Council. She&nbsp;
is a former Assistant Secretary of State for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:24.060
International Organization Affairs, which means&nbsp;
she worked with a lot of global institutions on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:30.360
lot of global issues, including the Arctic.
BRIMMER: One is the Eurasian landmass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.600
including Russia and the Nordic countries, and&nbsp;
the North American portion, which includes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:43.620
United States state of Alaska and Canada. The&nbsp;
North American side has coastal plains in parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:51.660
of Alaska. It has a series of islands in Canada.&nbsp;
And about 25,000 miles worth of coastline is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:58.200
Russia. The US has a significant amount of about&nbsp;
6,000 miles of coastline. So these are big spaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.840
but they're also very different spaces.
SIERRA: What is it like operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.300
in Arctic extremes?
RANDALL: One, it's cold. Two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:14.760
there's lots of ice, especially when you get up&nbsp;
into the ice cap regions, there's a beauty that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.880
exists there like no other place on Earth.
SIERRA: This is Captain Jeff Randall. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.160
the visiting military fellow for the U.S. Coast&nbsp;
Guard at the Council. Jeff has spent a great deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.860
of time in the Arctic, leading two Coast Guard&nbsp;
cutters and conducting fisheries law enforcement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.720
throughout the Pacific Arctic region. 
RANDALL: And knowing the whole time that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.940
operating up there that you're very alone. If&nbsp;
you're a ship, there's not a lot of gas stations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.920
If there's a person, there's not a lot of grocery&nbsp;
stores. If you're trying to operate there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.360
you have to think about your ability to sustain.&nbsp;
You have to basically take everything with you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.580
that you need to operate there. So you'd&nbsp;
have to stuff all that into your backpack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:01.140
so to speak, and carry that up there&nbsp;
with you, because at the same token,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.160
if you get in trouble there, if your car&nbsp;
breaks down, or in this case your ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.540
you need to be prepared to stay there for&nbsp;
a while or sustain yourself until rescue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:14.220
can come. It's a very challenging region to&nbsp;
operate in because you're so far from anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.680
so far from anywhere. 
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.660
So does anyone own the Arctic? 
BRIMMER: That's a very interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:29.760
question because you can think of the Arctic&nbsp;
as having sort of two parts. There's the part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.280
of the Arctic that is ocean, as we discussed,&nbsp;
that in a sense is owned by everybody as what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.200
was commonly called ‘common heritage of&nbsp;
mankind.’ That means that it's governed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.100
by the United Nations Convention on the&nbsp;
Law of the Sea, which pertains to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.240
rules of the open ocean. 
SIERRA: Arg the law of the sea!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:58.920
It may sound made up or like something pirates&nbsp;
adhere to, but it is in fact a very real treaty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:04.860
Basically, countries governing a shared region&nbsp;
like space or the world’s oceans gets pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.960
tricky. Regions often have conflicting&nbsp;
economic interests and claims. The Law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:15.240
of the Sea prescribes a framework for when&nbsp;
and how countries can establish rights at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:20.400
sea - this includes specific rules for marking&nbsp;
maritime boundaries, extracting resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.260
and navigating through foreign territory. It’s&nbsp;
important to note that the United States has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:30.780
never ratified the convention, though it does&nbsp;
abide by the norms it establishes. While the Law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:36.540
of the Sea is real, there is a lot of ambiguity&nbsp;
when it comes to what countries can and can’t do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:44.280
in open water. Things can get choppy!
BRIMMER: We have eight countries that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:53.760
territory in the Arctic region. Those include&nbsp;
Canada, Denmark, Finland, Iceland, Norway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:59.700
the Russian Federation, Sweden, and of course&nbsp;
the United States. I should note that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:04.620
Kingdom of Denmark has territory in this area&nbsp;
because of its relationship with Greenland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:13.020
An important new factor is that China has become&nbsp;
much more active in the Arctic. China is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:19.800
technically an Arctic country. However, China&nbsp;
has described itself as a near-Arctic nation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.360
because the very northern parts of Manchuria&nbsp;
are just below the Arctic Circle. 
&nbsp;

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:29.640
SIERRA: China has been quite busy in the&nbsp;
Arctic lately, doubling its investment in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:35.160
Arctic initiatives over the past decade, with a&nbsp;
focus on mineral extraction, scientific research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:42.360
and military research. President Xi Jinping has&nbsp;
expressed ambitions to become a “polar great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:48.660
power,” and he recently integrated the development&nbsp;
of a ‘Polar Silk Road’ into China’s existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:54.300
Belt and Road initiative. China’s presence&nbsp;
could change dynamics in the Arctic. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.300 --> 00:07:00.060
most Arctic nations are allies through NATO, and&nbsp;
tensions between Russia and NATO have been running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.160
high since the invasion of Ukraine. 
BRIMMER:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:12.060
Of the eight Arctic nations, five of them&nbsp;
have coastline directly on the Arctic Ocean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:19.860
For those countries, they have, as all countries&nbsp;
do, 12 nautical miles of territorial sea and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:25.200
200 miles of exclusive economic zone. So&nbsp;
when we think about who owns the Arctic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:30.300
there is the part that's since held as ‘common&nbsp;
heritage of mankind,’ the ocean in the middle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:35.940
and the territorial waters around it, and then&nbsp;
different nations have different rules about who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:40.920
owns land within their own countries, but we can&nbsp;
think about it both as common heritage surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:46.560
by territorial waters and territorial land.
SIERRA: While each state has its own territory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.500
under the international Convention of the Law of&nbsp;
the Sea, foreign vessels are allowed to travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:59.280
peacefully through those areas. The law also bans&nbsp;
weapons testing, spying, arbitrarily claiming new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:05.400
territory, and a number of other things. It also&nbsp;
protects resources in coastal territories from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:11.940
foreign extraction, bolstering states’ authority&nbsp;
over their zones of influence. The Law of Sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:18.420
provides structure to Arctic disputes, but there’s&nbsp;
still a lot of gray area when it comes to shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:24.960
waters. It’s easier to conduct business in claimed&nbsp;
territory. And that is why Arctic countries spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.000
a lot of time remapping their waters, in the&nbsp;
hope that they can extend their claims.
&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.460
RANDALL: A lot of the work that's going on up&nbsp;
there right now is to figure out the limits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:39.900
those continental shelves, to figure out&nbsp;
the limit of the claims, because there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:45.900
the potential for resources on the seabed or under&nbsp;
the sea floor, and potentially fisheries resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:51.240
that countries could lay claim to.
SIERRA: So what is going on up there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.200
now? Why are we talking about it?
BRIMMER: The Arctic region brings together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:02.160
so many important trends at the same time.&nbsp;
The first is that significant climate change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.840
is happening in the Arctic region. 
PBS: Warming in the Arctic is having dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:12.060
impacts now, with worse yet to come.
PBS: News for wildlife, native communities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:17.640
and global sea level rise is not good. 
 
Sky News: A fragile ecosystem is being lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:21.180
and the effects are reverberating&nbsp;
around the whole planet.
&nbsp;

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:27.300
BRIMMER: Now, every day we pick up the headlines&nbsp;
and we hear about climate change. That includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:33.480
rapid temperature change, and we're seeing that&nbsp;
in the Arctic. The Arctic may be warming up at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:39.600
least twice as fast as the rest of the globe,&nbsp;
maybe even three times as fast according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:46.140
some experts. Indeed, the National Oceanographic&nbsp;
and Atmospheric Administration known as NOAA says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:53.100
that the past seven years are collectively the&nbsp;
warmest on record. What we're seeing here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.100 --> 00:10:01.020
that because of the warming of the Arctic region,&nbsp;
you're having melting of the polar ice cap. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:09.000
conceivable that part of the Arctic Ocean could&nbsp;
be navigable in the near term. Some people think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:15.900
2050, some people 2040, some people think 2030.&nbsp;
Well, that means you might be able to sail ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:24.000
through an area that used to be ice. Therefore, we&nbsp;
see a variety of economic interests because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:31.080
environmental change that say perhaps we have&nbsp;
access to oil and gas, perhaps we can cut down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:37.140
the time of shipping between Europe and Asia.
SIERRA: Ships that currently navigate through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:41.940
Suez or Panama canals could cut their&nbsp;
shipping distances by up to half by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:46.560
traveling through the Arctic, which would&nbsp;
mean huge savings in fuel over time.
&nbsp;

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:52.020
WION: Typically, a ship from Rotterdam to Japan&nbsp;
would use the Suez Canal and take thirty days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:56.940
Once the Arctic ice melts, the new routes&nbsp;
emerge. The distance is going to shrink. 
 
&nbsp;

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:00.960
CNBC: Arctic sea routes are potentially&nbsp;
much more useful for avoiding the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.050
of bottlenecks that one would see in&nbsp;
either the Panama or Suez Canal. 
&nbsp;

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:07.680
CNBC: It’s really a question of&nbsp;
saving shipping costs, saving time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:13.260
versus that lack of predictability. 
BRIMMER: Let's take a moment and look at the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:21.300
major routes within the Arctic space. One is the&nbsp;
Northern Sea Route, which runs through Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:26.460
waters, as well as international waters. You can&nbsp;
think of the Northern Sea Route as one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:33.900
navigational routes that goes along the Eurasian&nbsp;
coast within the Arctic region. The other major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:41.460
route includes the Northwest Passage and goes&nbsp;
by the North American side of the Arctic.
&nbsp;

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:46.740
SIERRA: The Northern Sea Route, running&nbsp;
along the Russian Arctic coast, services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:51.960
most major Arctic ports and the rivers of&nbsp;
Siberia. It’s a part of what’s known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:58.320
the Northeast passage. The Northwest Passage,&nbsp;
running up the other side, connects Alaska to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:04.020
Greenland and passes through several contested&nbsp;
waterways. When it was first traversed in 1903,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:11.040
the Northwest Passage took three years to cross.&nbsp;
Today, it takes about 24 days. But these areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:17.520
can also be very dangerous. Ships can face deadly&nbsp;
rogue waves and surging storms as they cross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:22.980
and if something goes wrong, there often aren’t&nbsp;
any nearby ports to mount a rescue from.
&nbsp;

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:29.640
BRIMMER: Operating and navigating in the Arctic&nbsp;
is always challenging and 20 years from now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:36.060
there could still be significant hazards of pieces&nbsp;
of ice making it a challenging place in which to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:44.400
navigate. That said, the warming of the Arctic&nbsp;
does open up possibilities of greater exploration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:50.340
for oil, gas, and minerals. And so there are&nbsp;
companies and countries that are interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:55.620
in greater investment in these areas.
SIERRA: Right now, the Arctic supplies about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:01.260
10% of the world’s oil and a quarter of&nbsp;
its natural gas. And scientists predict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:07.020
that there is much more buried under the ice.
BRIMMER: Production from these areas are extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:12.240
important. We note that for Russia, which&nbsp;
is now currently under sanctions because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:18.900
its invasion of Ukraine, that the Arctic oil&nbsp;
that it produces is going to India and China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:23.880
which means it's still earning income from&nbsp;
that because it's able to get its oil out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:28.020
the Arctic region to other suppliers now that&nbsp;
they're no longer delivering to Europe. 
&nbsp;

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:34.740
SIERRA: The U.S.-held Arctic is also energy&nbsp;
rich. In fact, the Biden Administration recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:41.220
approved a new drilling project called Willow in&nbsp;
the Alaskan Arctic. Experts say it could reap over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:48.060
600 million barrels of crude oil over the next&nbsp;
thirty years. For people living in Arctic states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:53.280
these investments can be polarizing -&nbsp;
pitting local economic interests against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:58.080
cultural and environmental priorities.&nbsp;
But this is only the most recent example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:04.440
in a long history of Arctic extraction. 
BRIMMER: There are also enormous effects just of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:10.500
the environmental interest. It has direct impact&nbsp;
on the livelihoods of people, it has direct impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:17.040
on food sources for people, it has direct impact&nbsp;
on a large number of animals who live in the area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:21.660
including polar bears, whales, and others. Some&nbsp;
of the megafauna that people are familiar with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:27.600
that there could be fundamental changes in&nbsp;
the habitats for marine mammals as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:34.020
There could be important changes in fishing. Fish&nbsp;
and fish stocks, and the benefits of the sea are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:41.640
huge food sources. If water temperature changes,&nbsp;
the movement of fish and other foods would also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:51.240
change. In addition to that, you have people whose&nbsp;
homes, and livelihoods, and culture, would be lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:59.400
as communities literally slip into the sea. 
SIERRA: There are nearly half a million indigenous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:05.520
people living in the Arctic circle, spanning more&nbsp;
than 40 different ethnic groups. Fishing, hunting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:10.320
and whaling are the main ways these communities&nbsp;
have survived in this harsh region for over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:16.620
15,000 years. But climate change and commercial&nbsp;
ventures have led to a shift in migration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:22.740
patterns, impacting native food sources. And given&nbsp;
many native communities live without access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:29.460
running water or a functioning sewer system, many&nbsp;
groups may soon be forced to relocate. Alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:36.660
Arctic natives are 3.5 million non-indigenous&nbsp;
hunters, herders, and city locals, who also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:42.660
rely on the region to raise their families, farm,&nbsp;
and run businesses. These two groups often find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:48.960
themselves at the mercy of more powerful forces -&nbsp;
a changing climate, and a group of large nations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:56.100
vying to control the region’s future.
BRIMMER: As we said earlier, there are about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.100 --> 00:16:02.820
4 million people living in the Arctic. About 2&nbsp;
million of them are in Russia, and about 500,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:09.000
are Indigenous people. Also, from the US&nbsp;
perspective, have to recognize that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:15.360
environmental challenges are also highlighting&nbsp;
already existing challenges faced by Alaska&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:21.240
native communities. There are at least 31&nbsp;
Alaska native communities that do not have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:28.260
reliable running water in their homes. 60% of&nbsp;
Alaska native communities have been described as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:35.760
environmentally threatened by climate change. And&nbsp;
finally, working in Alaska is challenging. Only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:41.940
about 20% of the State of Alaska is accessible by&nbsp;
roads. Therefore, boats and aircraft are crucial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:47.580
for transportation. So that this important climate&nbsp;
change is happening in an area that is complex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:55.020
very large, and already difficult to work in,&nbsp;
making the effort more challenging, and the need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.020 --> 00:17:01.080
for cooperation, all that more important.
SIERRA: But instead of cooperation, as the ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:06.000
continues to melt, the race for precious&nbsp;
resources is going to heat up competition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:11.880
even further in the open Arctic. And because the&nbsp;
stakeholders are either NATO-aligned countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:17.820
or NATO rivals, the chances for that economic&nbsp;
competition to spill over into military tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:23.700
has many analysts concerned. Already, Norway&nbsp;
and Russia are butting heads over control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:29.400
of the Norwegian Svalbard islands in the Arctic&nbsp;
Circle. Russia is asserting that a historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:35.820
right to mine there is being blocked by Ukraine&nbsp;
War-related sanctions, and Moscow is threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:41.100
“retaliatory measures” if Norway doesn’t let&nbsp;
them resume their operations in the region.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:48.780
Situations like this can get tense - fast. 
SIERRA: And what about the military side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:52.620
of things? Is there a history of U.S.&nbsp;
military presence in the arctic? 
&nbsp;

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:57.720
RANDALL: If you think about post-World War&nbsp;
II, we entered into a period of a Cold War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:03.840
where you basically had Russia and the US&nbsp;
at odds with each other. During that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:08.340
and because of World War II, where the&nbsp;
Japanese had actually invaded the Aleutians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:14.220
you saw a buildup of military presence&nbsp;
through early warning sites, through basing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:21.180
through other infrastructure in the Arctic region.&nbsp;
As the Cold War progressed, and as we ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:27.240
coming out of the Cold War in the early '90s, we&nbsp;
let a lot of that infrastructure atrophy. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:33.300
we still have some early detection, we still have&nbsp;
some presence up there, but some of the basing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:39.540
and some of the infrastructure we let atrophy&nbsp;
over time. Then, as you saw Russia begin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:46.020
militarize their Arctic, we began rethinking about&nbsp;
our operations, our ability to sustain operations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:52.560
and our ability to project military power from&nbsp;
the Arctic region. And so, that's brought us back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:56.940
to an era where we're starting to think about&nbsp;
infrastructure investments, thinking about our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:03.000
operations, and thinking about what investments&nbsp;
we need to make in the regions to ensure that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:09.180
can protect our sovereignty and project power, if&nbsp;
needed, if there were to be conflict across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:15.060
Bering Strait or other areas in the region. 
SIERRA: As it stands, Russia is the dominant power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:21.120
in the Arctic, exceeding NATO’s number of military&nbsp;
bases by a third. And in the past two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:28.080
Russia has upgraded nearly a dozen Soviet-era air&nbsp;
strips at its Northernmost base and spent millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:33.600
on artillery improvements for its Northern Fleet&nbsp;
- the largest icebreaker armada in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:39.540
And these are just some of the moves that have&nbsp;
made other Arctic nations wary when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:42.100
Russia’s ambitions for the region. 
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.560
Let's talk about national security. What are we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:52.680
worried about when we look at the Arctic? 
BRIMMER: The Arctic poses several challenges for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:58.140
national security. For one, half of the entire&nbsp;
coastline in the United States is in Alaska.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:04.500
So first and foremost, you have just the normal&nbsp;
defense of the United States borders. In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:09.480
the United States is aware, of course,&nbsp;
that Russia has been upgrading its military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:14.940
installations in the region, and it has&nbsp;
been reinvesting in its infrastructure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:23.100
We saw after the end of the Cold War a reduction&nbsp;
in tensions, a greater spirit of cooperation, of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:28.560
which the Arctic Council is a good example.
SIERRA: The Arctic Council is an international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:34.200
forum that includes representatives from&nbsp;
all 8 arctic nations, as well as from local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:40.860
communities. Formed in 1996, the Arctic Council&nbsp;
brought together Russia and its former Cold War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:45.900
adversaries in the hope that they could cooperate&nbsp;
to manage a region that was important to all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:52.680
them. For years the Arctic Council was a symbol of&nbsp;
fruitful cooperation between rivals, but now the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:59.280
organization’s fate is unclear. Russia is chairing&nbsp;
the council for the next two years and very little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:05.280
cooperation is expected in the near term. 
SIERRA: Okay. So where do you see us going as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:07.860
a nation in the Arctic?
RANDALL: I think what you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:11.880
going to see is an area where we're going to&nbsp;
have to make some investment. I think you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:16.080
going to have to make some investment&nbsp;
not only in our ability to operate up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:21.180
there through icebreaking capability in&nbsp;
ships. I think you're going to see some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:27.240
need for investment up there to make sure&nbsp;
you have the ability to operate in a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:32.220
continuous and sustained basis. I think&nbsp;
you're going to see probably some more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:40.740
military exercises up there, and not necessarily&nbsp;
as a counter to what we see in Russia, but to show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:46.020
that we can operate up there and make sure that&nbsp;
we are making the right investments to ensure our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:51.840
ability to operate in the region as needed.
BRIMMER: There's several different ways of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:57.480
present in the Arctic. One of them is physical&nbsp;
presence. So for example, the Russian Federation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:03.000
because it has the longest coastline within the&nbsp;
Arctic region, has an important physical presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:08.520
in the Arctic by virtue of its own geography.&nbsp;
The Nordic states, again, because they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:13.260
long coastlines have an important presence in&nbsp;
the Arctic, but there are other ways of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:19.980
present in the Arctic. And one of them is an&nbsp;
area which is undergoing change at the moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:27.240
Both Finland and Sweden have applied to join&nbsp;
NATO. This would mean if they're approved by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:33.540
all the NATO states, that all of the members of&nbsp;
the Arctic region, except for Russia, would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:40.440
NATO members. So being part of an alliance is also&nbsp;
part of being present in the Arctic. In a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:46.500
NATO becomes more present in the Arctic.
SIERRA: How much energy should we be focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:51.120
on the Arctic at this point?
RANDALL: Well, I would tell you it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:56.340
priority amongst priorities. And anytime you talk&nbsp;
about priorities amongst priorities in government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:00.360
the issue that is most close at hand&nbsp;
tends to get the most attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:06.120
The Arctic is a bit of a slow burn&nbsp;
issue and it's going to be there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:11.760
but if you don't act sooner rather than later,&nbsp;
you may find yourself closer to crisis, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:16.620
with climate change and other impacts to the&nbsp;
indigenous communities or the region in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:22.560
because you didn't pay enough attention to it&nbsp;
now. Right now, a lot of our national attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:28.500
is focused on China-Taiwan, Russia-Ukraine.&nbsp;
We're only one another terrorism event from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:35.160
refocusing on counterterrorism. And so, then&nbsp;
where does the Arctic fall? So I think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:40.920
requires attention. I think it requires some&nbsp;
advocacy. I think it requires some investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:48.780
and that needs to be on a sustained basis instead&nbsp;
of episodic injects to make sure that we're ready,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:52.800
postured to achieve those goals that are&nbsp;
in the National Arctic Strategy.
&nbsp;

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:58.020
BRIMMER: It also should be remembered that&nbsp;
the Arctic in some ways is also part of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.020 --> 00:24:04.500
national spirit. Living in the Arctic has had&nbsp;
an important cultural role for Canada, Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:09.240
and the Nordic countries. And I would suggest&nbsp;
that for the United States, one thing to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:13.560
about is to broaden our understanding of how&nbsp;
we think about the US in the world. We think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:17.640
of the Transatlantic, Trans-Pacific, and the&nbsp;
Gulf. We think of our important neighbors to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:23.580
the North in Canada. But even beyond that, the&nbsp;
United States is also an Arctic nation, so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:29.520
Arctic Ocean is America's fourth coast.
SIERRA: We live in an era of great power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:36.000
competition, and part of that competition could&nbsp;
play out in a melting North pole. Caught in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:40.680
whirlwind are indigenous people and local&nbsp;
workers who depend on this changing region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:46.740
for their livelihoods, and a wealth of flora&nbsp;
and fauna fighting to survive. Like space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:53.100
the Arctic is a region that we all wish could&nbsp;
forever remain peaceful. A shared territory for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:59.880
scientific inquiry. But the oil beneath the waves,&nbsp;
and the perilous but valuable sea routes that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:05.940
opening up are a tempting prize that could&nbsp;
make that cooperation difficult to maintain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

